Notebook[{
Cell[TextData[{
  "Week 14   Projective Geometry\n",
  StyleBox["Return to Euclidean Geometry",
    FontSize->18]
}], "Title",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True,
  FontSize->36],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["\[Copyright] Dana Scott 1993/1997/1998",
    FontSize->16,
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["\nNotebook  last edited: ",
    FontSize->16],
  StyleBox["30 November 1998",
    FontSize->16,
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Subtitle",
  FontSize->18],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Euclidean Geometry", "Section"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Euclidean transformations",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Subsection"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Transforming the line at infinity and the circular points", 
  "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Projective geometry is a generalization of ordinary geometry \[LongDash] \
one might say it is a ",
  StyleBox["completion",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " by adjoining the line at infinity and by using complex numbers.  But \
there is a reverse procedure as well: deriving the Euclidean world from the \
projective."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "In order to understand the connection between projective and Euclidean \
geometry, we note first that we previoisly verified algebraically that ",
  StyleBox["circles",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " are exactly the conics which pass through the two, fixed, complex, \
so-called ",
  StyleBox["circular points at infinity",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", namely the two points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(u \[PlusMinus] I\ v\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Hence, we define:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Definition.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  ",
  StyleBox["Euclidean transformations",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " are the projective transformations which map circles to circles.  There \
are two kinds: the ",
  StyleBox["direct",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " transformations that the leave the points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(u \[PlusMinus] I\ v\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " fixed, and the ",
  StyleBox["opposite",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " transformations that reverse the two points."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell["It follows at once that we have:", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " The Euclidean transformations form a ",
  StyleBox["subgroup",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the group of all projectiove transformations, and the opposite \
transformations form a smaller subgroup.  The product of two opposite \
Euclidean transformations is a direct transformation, the inverse of an \
opposite transformation is opposite, the product of a direct and an opposite \
transformation is an opposite transformation."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Comment.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Technically we can say that the direct transformations form a ",
  StyleBox["normal subgroup",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the group of Euclidean transformations of ",
  StyleBox["index 2",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Every ",
  StyleBox["direct",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " Euclidean transformation is equivalent to one of the form:\n\t\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{"(", GridBox[{
              {\(a\_1\), \(a\_2\), "0"},
              {\(-a\_2\), \(a\_1\), "0"},
              {\(c\_1\), \(c\_2\), "1"}
              }], ")"}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "\nThe ",
  StyleBox["opposite",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "transformations are those equivalent to one of the form:\n\t\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{"(", GridBox[{
              {\(a\_1\), \(a\_2\), "0"},
              {\(a\_2\), \(-a\_1\), "0"},
              {\(c\_1\), \(c\_2\), "1"}
              }], ")"}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]]
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  First let us take a general matrix defining a projective \
transformation:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ClearAll[M, a, b, c, Subscript]; \n
    M = {{a\_1, a\_2, a\_3}, {b\_1, b\_2, b\_3}, {c\_1, c\_2, c\_3}}; \)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The following quantities must be ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["0",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", if the transformation leaves ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(u + I\ v\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " fixed:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(PE[\(TP[M]\)[u + I\ v], u + I\ v] // CL\) // ExpandAll\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We can say the same about ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(u - I\ v\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ":"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(PE[\(TP[M]\)[u - I\ v], u - I\ v] // CL\) // ExpandAll\)], "Input"],

Cell["We can then see what this means:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(sol = 
      Solve[{\(-I\)\ a\_3 + b\_3 == 0, a\_3 + I\ b\_3 == 0, 
          I\ a\_1 - a\_2 - b\_1 - I\ b\_2 == 0, I\ a\_3 + b\_3 == 0, 
          a\_3 - I\ b\_3 == 0, \(-I\)\ a\_1 - a\_2 - b\_1 + I\ b\_2 == 0}, 
        Reverse[{a\_1, a\_2, a\_3, b\_1, b\_2, b\_3, c\_1, c\_2, c\_3}]]\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell["\<\
So here is the form the matrix has to have, along with its \
determinant:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(MatrixForm[M /. sol\[LeftDoubleBracket]1\[RightDoubleBracket]]\), 
    \(Det[%] // Factor\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Since the transformation has to be non singular, we see ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(c\_3 \[NotEqual] 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Thus, we can ",
  StyleBox["divide",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " by that scalar obtaining the first stated form.  "
}], "Text"],

Cell["We turn next to the opposite transformations:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(PE[\(TP[M]\)[u - I\ v], u + I\ v] // CL\) // ExpandAll\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(PE[\(TP[M]\)[u + I\ v], u - I\ v] // CL\) // ExpandAll\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(sol = 
      Solve[{\(-I\)\ a\_3 - b\_3 == 0, a\_3 - I\ b\_3 == 0, 
          I\ a\_1 - a\_2 + b\_1 + I\ b\_2 == 0, I\ a\_3 - b\_3 == 0, 
          a\_3 + I\ b\_3 == 0, \(-I\)\ a\_1 - a\_2 + b\_1 - I\ b\_2 == 0}, 
        Reverse[{a\_1, a\_2, a\_3, b\_1, b\_2, b\_3, c\_1, c\_2, c\_3}]]\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell["\<\
So here is the form the matrix has to have, along with its \
determinant:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(MatrixForm[M /. sol\[LeftDoubleBracket]1\[RightDoubleBracket]]\), 
    \(Det[%] // Factor\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "By the same argument as before, we can divide by ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(c\_3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
Look at a typical direct transformation, a special opposite one, \
and their product:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(M = {{a\_1, a\_2, 0}, {\(-a\_2\), a\_1, 0}, {c\_1, c\_2, 1}}; \n
    R = {{0, 1, 0}, {1, 0, 0}, {0, 0, 1}}; \n\nM . R // MatrixForm\n\), 
    \(M . R . R // MatrixForm\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "In general, multiplication of a matrix by ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["R",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " interchanges the first ans second rows of the matrix.  For the special \
case of Euclidean transformations, we have checked:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  The mapping ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(M\  \[Rule] \ M . R\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " takes every (matrix representing a) direct Euclidean transformation to an \
opposite one, and every opposite one to a direct one.  The transformation \
composed with itself is the identity."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Question.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  What is the ",
  StyleBox["geometric",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["meaning",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the transformation represented by ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["R",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "?"
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell["\<\
The answer is left to the reader (but we shall return to it).  \
Another question that is interesting to answer is:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Question.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  By definition, Euclidean transformations leave the ",
  StyleBox["pair",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({u + I\ v, u - I\ v}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " fixed (as a set) and hence the line ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["z",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " at infinity fixed.  (Why?) Which of the Euclidean transformations leave ",
  
  StyleBox["all ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "points at infinity fixed?"
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Answer:",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " Inasmuch as ",
  StyleBox["two",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " points are already fixed by direct transformations, it is sufficient to \
see when they leave another point fixed.  (Why?)"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(PE[\(TP[M]\)[u], u] // CL\) // ExpandAll\)], "Input"],

Cell["So, here is the form we seek:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(M /. a\_2 \[Rule] 0 // MatrixForm\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "That is the answer.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.F.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
These transformations must form another subgroup.  (Why?)   Are \
there other, related subgroups?\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Question.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Which Euclidean transformations leave ",
  StyleBox["all ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "points at infinity fixed, as well as ",
  StyleBox["the",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["origin",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "?"
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Answer:",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  We check what happens to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["w",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ":"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(PE[\(TP[M]\)[w], w] // CL\) // ExpandAll\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
So, here is the form we seek (continuing on from the previous \
question):\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(M\_1 = M /. {a\_2 \[Rule] 0, c\_1 \[Rule] 0, c\_2 \[Rule] 0}; \t\n
    M\_1 // MatrixForm\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Let us check what this kind of transformation does to an ordinary \
point in the plane.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(TP[M\_1]\)[r\ u + s\ v + w]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Aside from the irritating order ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " writes things in, we see that this just means that each coordinate is \
multiplied by the same factor (namely, ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ").  And this factor has to be ",
  StyleBox["non zero",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  (Why?)  So this is a uniform ",
  StyleBox["change of scale",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " or a ",
  StyleBox["similitude",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["around the origin",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  The subgroup of such similitudes is isomorphic to the ",
  StyleBox["multiplicative group",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the non-zero scalars.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.F.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Question.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Which Euclidean transformations leave ",
  StyleBox["the",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["origin",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " fixed?"
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Answer:",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  Just as before, we find a simplification in the third row:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PE[\(TP[M]\)[w], w] // CL\)], "Input"],

Cell["So, here is the form we seek :", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(M\_2 = M /. {c\_1 \[Rule] 0, c\_2 \[Rule] 0}; \t\n
    M\_2 // MatrixForm\), 
    \(Det[M\_2]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "What is the ",
  StyleBox["meaning",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of such transformations?  If we combine ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(M\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "with a suitable similitude:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(M\_3 = Inverse[M\_1 /. a\_1 \[Rule] \@\(a\_1\%2 + a\_2\%2\)]; \n
    M\_3 // MatrixForm\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We get a matrix with determinant ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["1",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Det[M\_2 . M\_3] // Together\), 
    \(M\_2 . M\_3 // MatrixForm\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This is the matrix for a ",
  StyleBox["rotation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " through angle ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{"ArcTan", "[", 
          RowBox[{
            SubscriptBox[
              StyleBox["a",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], "2"], "/", \(a\_1\)}], "]"}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "about the origin.  So, the transformations leaving the origin fixed are \
represented by a combination of a rotation and a similitude.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.F.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Warning.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  The last conclusion may be a little hard to interpret for ",
  StyleBox["complex",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " matrices. "
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Definition.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  In general, a ",
  StyleBox["rotation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " is a direct Euclidean transformation of determinant 1 leaving a point \
fixed (the ",
  StyleBox["center",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the rotation)."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell["\<\
An interesting question is to determine when the composition of two \
rotations is again a rotation.  But we shall not pursue that here.\
\>", 
  "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Question.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Are there ",
  StyleBox["other",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " obvious subgroups?"
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Answer:",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  Here is one:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(T = {{1, 0, 0}, {0, 1, 0}, {c\_1, c\_2, 1}}; \n
    U = {{1, 0, 0}, {0, 1, 0}, {d\_1, d\_2, 1}}; \nT // MatrixForm\), 
    \(U // MatrixForm\)}], "Input"],

Cell["And we find:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Inverse[T] // MatrixForm\), 
    \(T . U // MatrixForm\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "So we have a ",
  StyleBox["subgroup",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "!  What is the meaning of these transformations?  Let us check how they \
transform points:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(TP[T]\)[r\ u + s\ v + w]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Such transformations are just ",
  StyleBox["translations",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " by a vector.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.F.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Note that if we think of linear combinations of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["u",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["v",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " as being Euclidean vectors, then we get the transformations for \
translations as projective transformation as well by transforming \
quadrangles:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(vect = \(c\_1\) u + \(c\_2\) v; \n
    MQP[{u + I\ v, u - I\ v, u + w, \ v + w}, \n
        \t\ \ \ \ \ \ \ {u + I\ v, u - I\ v, u + w\  + vect, 
          v + w\  + vect}] // MatrixForm\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(MQP[{w, u + w, \ v + w, u + v + w}, \ \ \n
        \t\ \ \ \ \ \ \ {w, u + w, \ v + w, u + v + w} + vect] // MatrixForm
      \)], "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Real geometry and orientation", "Subsubsection"],

Cell["\<\
We consider again the direct and opposite Euclidean \
transformations:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(ClearAll[a, b, c, M, R, Subscript]; \n
    M = {{a\_1, a\_2, 0}, {\(-a\_2\), a\_1, 0}, {c\_1, c\_2, 1}}; \n
    R = {{0, 1, 0}, {1, 0, 0}, {0, 0, 1}}; \nM // MatrixForm\), 
    \(M . R // MatrixForm\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Question. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Which Euclidean transformations map real points to real points? "
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Answer:",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " Check the origin first under ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["M",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ":"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(TP[M]\)[w]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Thus, both ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(c\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(c\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " have to be real, if real points are to be mapped to real points..  Let us \
check another real point: "
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(TP[M]\)[u + w]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Thus, both ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " are real.  Since ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["R",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " obviously maps real points to real points, we can say the ",
  StyleBox["real",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " Euclidean transformations are those that can be represented by a ",
  StyleBox["real",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["matrix",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  These transforms obviously form a subgroup.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.F.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "What is the meaning of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["R",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "?  It is clear that it leaves the origin fixed and interchanges the ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "- and ",
  StyleBox["y",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axes.  More generally we see from the computation "
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(TP[R]\)[r\ u + s\ v + w]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["R",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " interchanges the ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "- and ",
  StyleBox["y",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-coordinates of every point.  Thus we have the transformation of ",
  StyleBox["reflection",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " about the diagonal line ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(x - y\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Indeed, we find:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(TL[R]/@{x, y, z, x - y}\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Another interesting exercise \[LongDash] which, alas, we do not have the \
time to solve explicitly \[LongDash] is to find the formula for ",
  StyleBox["all",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " reflections (about any line) and to prove:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  The ",
  StyleBox["opposite",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " Euclidean transformations of determinant ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["1",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " are exactly the compositions of a ",
  StyleBox["reflection",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " in a line and a ",
  StyleBox["translation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " parallel to that line."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "As a reflection is a key example of an ",
  StyleBox["orientation reversing ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " transformation, we can see that ",
  StyleBox["all",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the opposite transformations are orientation reversing.  The direct \
transformations are therefore the ",
  StyleBox["orientation preserving",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " transformations.  It can also be proved that:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  The ",
  StyleBox["direct",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " Euclidean transformations of determinant ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["1",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " are either ",
  StyleBox["rotations",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " about a center or ",
  StyleBox["translations",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The ",
  StyleBox["similitudes",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " are all orientation preserving, and so we see there are a number of \
subgroups to be singled out here ",
  StyleBox["including",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " those that preserve real points, giving us the transformations of \
ordinary geometry."
}], "Text"],

Cell["Here is an example of a rotation and a reflection:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(ClearAll[M, R, Subscript]; \n
    M = {{Cos[\[Pi]/3], Sin[\[Pi]/3], 0}, {\(-Sin[\[Pi]/3]\), Cos[\[Pi]/3], 
          0}, {3, 2, 1}}; \nR = {{0, 1, 0}, {1, 0, 0}, {0, 0, 1}}; \n
    MatrixForm[M]\), 
    \(MatrixForm[R]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now, if we look at a configuration of lines and their transforms under ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["M",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and under ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox["M",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[".",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["R",
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]],
  ", we can see the first is orientation preserving and the second is \
orientation reversing."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(l\_1 = x - 5  y - z; \nl\_2 = x + y - z; \nl\_3 = x + 2\ y - 3\ z; \n
    ImplicitPlot[{l\_1 == 0, l\_2 == 0, l\_3 == 0, \n\t\t
          \(TL[M]\)[l\_1] == 0, \n\t\t\(TL[M]\)[l\_2] == 0, \n\t\t
          \(TL[M]\)[l\_3] == 0} /. z \[Rule] 1, \n
      \t{x, \(-2\), 5}, {y, \(-2\), 5}, \n
      PlotStyle \[Rule] 
        \ {{Thickness[0.005], Black}, {Thickness[0.005], Black}, {
            Thickness[0.005], Black}, {Thickness[0.005], Red}, \n
          \t\t\t{Thickness[0.005], Red}, {Thickness[0.005], Red}}]; \)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(l\_1 = x - 5  y - z; \nl\_2 = x + y - z; \nl\_3 = x + 2\ y - 3\ z; \n
    ImplicitPlot[{l\_1 == 0, l\_2 == 0, l\_3 == 0, \n\t\t
          \(TL[M . R]\)[l\_1] == 0, \n\t\t\(TL[M . R]\)[l\_2] == 0, \n\t\t
          \(TL[M . R]\)[l\_3] == 0} /. z \[Rule] 1, \n
      \t{x, \(-2\), 5}, {y, \(-2\), 5}, \n
      PlotStyle \[Rule] 
        \ {{Thickness[0.005], Black}, {Thickness[0.005], Black}, {
            Thickness[0.005], Black}, {Thickness[0.005], Red}, \n
          \t\t\t{Thickness[0.005], Red}, {Thickness[0.005], Red}}]; \)], 
  "Input"],

Cell["\<\
We cannot make the red and black triangles in the second picture \
coincide by \"sliding\" alone:  we have to turn one of them over.\
\>", "Text"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Some definitions and constructions",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Subsection"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Perpendicular lines and congruent segments", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Consider the ",
  StyleBox["involution",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " on the line at infinity defined by finding the ",
  StyleBox["harmonic conjugate",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of a point with respect to the two circular points.  Here is a \
definition:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ClearAll[a, b, c, Subscript]; \n
    XP[u + I\ v, u - I\ v, a\ u + b\ v, \(-1\)]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The common factor ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["I",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " does not matter, and we are just looking at a version of the linear \
transformation ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\ u + b\ v \[Rule] b\ u - a\ v\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", which amounts to an ",
  StyleBox["involution",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " on the line at infinity.   "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This transformation at infinity can be ",
  StyleBox["transferred",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to any pencil through any point in the usual way.  Here is the result for \
the pencil at the origin:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(XL[PE[\ w, u + I\ v], PE[I\ w, u - I\ v], a\ x\  + \ b\ y, \(-1\)]\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Again, the common factor ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["I",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " does not matter, and we are looking at the linear transformation ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\ x + b\ y \[Rule] b\ x - a\ y\ \),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Considering the ",
  StyleBox["slopes",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of lines, we find that the ",
  StyleBox["geometric meaning",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of this transformation is that any line through the origin is transformed \
to the corresponding ",
  StyleBox["perpendicular",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " line.  We will have to discuss the full justification for the following \
definition; but, so far, we can say it works for ordinary lines."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Definition.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " Two lines (neither of which are at infinity) are ",
  StyleBox["perpendicular",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " if, and only if, ",
  StyleBox["either",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " they pass through the ",
  StyleBox["same",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " circular point at infinity ",
  StyleBox["or",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the circular points separate their intersections with the line at \
infinity ",
  StyleBox["harmonically",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The point of looking at the construction of a perpendicular by means of \
crossratios is that \[LongDash] once we have fixed the circular points at \
infinity \[LongDash] the construction is ",
  StyleBox["definable in projective geometry",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  But, we also want to make this idea into a program (or construction) \
for ",
  StyleBox["finding",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the perpendicular to a line passing through a point.  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Unfortunately, the command ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(XP[u + I\ v, u - I\ v, a\ u + b\ v, \(-1\)]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " does not work for the third point being one of the first two (circular) \
points, so we use the projective transformation of rotation through ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(\[Pi]\/2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to as an expedient to gain the same effect.  This transformation leaves \
the circular points ",
  StyleBox["fixed",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", as desired.   Here follows the text of the ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " code.  (The command itself in defined in ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(Implementations . nb\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".)\n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        \(Perp[p_, l_] := \n\t
          Tidy[PE[p, \ 
              \(TP[{{0, 1, 0}, {\(-1\), 0, 0}, {0, 0, 1}}]\)[PE[l, z]]]]\),
        FontSize->13,
        FontWeight->"Bold"]],
    Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]]
}], "Text"],

Cell["Here is a test:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Perp[r\ u + s\ v + w, a\ x\  + \ b\ y\  + c\ z]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "It has the right slope.  Here is the ",
  StyleBox["foot",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the perpendicular:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Tidy[PE[a\ x + b\ y + c\ z, Perp[r\ u + s\ v + w, a\ x + b\ y + c\ z]]]
      \), 
    \(PE[%, r\ u + s\ v + w] // Factor\), 
    \(PD[a\ x + b\ y + c\ z, r\ u + s\ v + w]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We see the foot of the perpendicular is the ",
  StyleBox["same point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " as the given point ",
  StyleBox["if, and only if,",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the point ",
  StyleBox["lies on",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the given line \[LongDash] as would be expected.  Here is a picture:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(a = 4; b = 1; c = \(-1\); \n
    ImplicitPlot[{a\ x + b\ y + c\ z == 0, 
          Perp[u - v + w, a\ x + b\ y + c\ z] == 0} /. z \[Rule] 1, \n
      \t{x, \(-3\), 3}, {y, \(-3\), 3}, 
      PlotStyle 
        \[Rule] {{Thickness[0.007], Black}, {Thickness[0.007], Red}}]; \)], 
  "Input"],

Cell["We have to watch out for complex coefficients! ", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Question.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "   Can you show how complex coordinates differ from the real case?"
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Answer:  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "Consider the line joining the origin to one of the circular points."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PE[w, u + I\ v]\)], "Input"],

Cell["Now, erect the perpendicular:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Perp[w, \(-I\)\ x + y] I // Tidy\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "What?!  It is the same line!?  Under our definitions, the line  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(\(-I\)\ x + y\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "  (which is equivalent to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(\ x + I\ y\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ") is infact ",
  StyleBox["perpendicular to itself",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[". ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " Why?  In the reals, the quantity ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\^2 + b\^2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is never zero, unless both ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["a",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["b",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " are zero; in the complex numbers, it is another story.  This algebraic \
fact is at the basis of the situation we found.  Here is the calculation that \
shows why:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Perp[w, a\ x + b\ y]\), 
    \(PE[%, a\ x + b\ y] // Factor\)}], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Now, drop a perpenduclar from another point (not at infinity):\
\>",
   "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ClearAll[a, b, c]; \n
    Perp[a\ u + b\ v + w, \(-I\)\ x + y]\ I // Tidy\)], "Input"],

Cell["These lines are are all parallel (of course):", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PE[\(-I\)\ x + y, \(-I\)\ x + y + \((I\ a - b)\)\ z]/\((I\ a - b)\) // 
      Together\)], "Input"],

Cell["And they are perpendicular to themselves:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ClearAll[a, b, c]; \n
    Perp[a\ u + b\ v + w, \(-I\)\ x + y + \((I\ a - b)\)\ z]\ I // Tidy\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This shows how complex coefficients create self-perpendicular lines.  \
These lines are the lines passing through one of the circular points.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.F.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Note.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  From the dictionary: ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " = ",
  StyleBox["quod erat demonstrandum",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " = which was to be demonstrated or proved; ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.F.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " = ",
  StyleBox["quod erat faciendum ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "= which was to be done or constructed."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We can also use the construction of the foot of the perpendicular to make \
a ",
  StyleBox["construction",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the ",
  StyleBox["reflection around any line",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  Here is one way: take the line, take a point, drop the perendicular, go \
over an equal amount to the other side of the line.  If we do this for two \
special points, we determine the reflection.  Here is an example:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(l = 2\ x\  - \ 3  y - 4  z; \n
    refl = MQP[{u + I\ v, u - I\ v, u + w, v + w}, \n
        \t\ \ \ \ \ \ \ {u - I\ v, u + I\ v, \n\t\t
          XP[PE[z, Perp[u + w, l]], PE[l, Perp[u + w, l]], u + w, \(-1\)], \n
          \t\ \ XP[PE[z, Perp[v + w, l]], PE[l, Perp[v + w, l]], v + w, 
            \(-1\)]}]; \n\nrefl // MatrixForm\n\), 
    \(\(TL[refl]\)[l]\), 
    \(m = 4  x - 2  y - 1  z; \n\(TL[refl]\)[m]\n\), 
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[{l == 0, m == 0, \n\t\t\(TL[refl]\)[m] == 0} /. 
        z \[Rule] 1, \n\t{x, \(-3\), 3}, {y, \(-3\), 3}, 
      PlotStyle 
        \[Rule] {{Thickness[0.007], Black}, {Thickness[0.007], Red}, \n
          \t\ \ \ \t{Thickness[0.007], Red}}]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The black line above is called the ",
  StyleBox["axis",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the reflection.  Note that the red lines make equal and opposite ",
  StyleBox["angles",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " with the axis.  (This corresponds to the physical idea of the reflection \
of a light ray off a mirror.)"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Question.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Can the above example be made into a program?"
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Answer:",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  The problem comes when the line through the two points (we have just \
used ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({u + w, v + w}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ") is perpendicular to the given line.  Another problem is that the given \
line should not be ",
  StyleBox["self-perpendicular",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  An easier procedure than the above to determine the transformation uses \
the fact that all the points on an axis of reflection ",
  StyleBox["remain fixed",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  If we can find two distinct points on the line, we just leave them \
fixed.  Since the line cannot pass through the circular points, if we just \
find one point ",
  StyleBox["off",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the given line, we can connect that to the two circular points and get \
two points ",
  StyleBox["on",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the line.  Here follows the suggested program.  (The command itself in \
defined in ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(Implementations . nb\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".)"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox[
          \(Refl[l_] := 
            \((MQP[{u + I\ v, u - I\ v, #1, #2}, \n
                    \t\t\t\[NonBreakingSpace]\[NonBreakingSpace]\
\[NonBreakingSpace]\[NonBreakingSpace]\[NonBreakingSpace]\[NonBreakingSpace]\
\[NonBreakingSpace]\[NonBreakingSpace]\[NonBreakingSpace]\[NonBreakingSpace]\
\[NonBreakingSpace]\[NonBreakingSpace]\[NonBreakingSpace]{u - I\ v, 
                      u + I\ v, #1, #2}]&)\)@@
              \(({PE[l, PE[u + I\ v, #]], PE[l, PE[u - I\ v, #]]}&)\)[
                \((If[#3 \[NotEqual] 0, w, #1\ u + #2\ v + w]&)\)@@CL[l]]\),
          FontSize->14,
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[";",
          FontSize->15,
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]],
    Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]]
}], "Text"],

Cell["Let us make some tests:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Refl[x - y] // MatrixForm\), 
    \(Refl[x + y] // MatrixForm\)}], "Input"],

Cell["So far so good.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Refl[x - I\ y] // MatrixForm\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Opps!  Wrong kind of input!  That line passes through a circular \
point:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(PD[x - I\ y, #]&\)/@{u + I\ v, u - I\ v}\)], "Input"],

Cell["Look at related lines:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Refl[x - a\ I\ y] // MatrixForm\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "To get well defined results here, the scalar ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["a",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " must not be ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(\[PlusMinus]1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Those values give the two lines through the origin also through a \
circular point.  Here is another test:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(l = 2\ x\  + \ 3  y - 4  z; \nRefl[l] // MatrixForm\n\), 
    \(\(TL[Refl[l]]\)[l]\), 
    \(m = 4  x - 5  y + 3  z; \n\(TL[Refl[l]]\)[m]\n\), 
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[{l == 0, m == 0, \n\t\t\(TL[Refl[l]]\)[m] == 0} /. 
        z \[Rule] 1, \n\t{x, \(-3\), 3}, {y, \(-3\), 3}, 
      PlotStyle 
        \[Rule] {{Thickness[0.007], Black}, {Thickness[0.007], Red}, \n
          \t\ \ \ \t{Thickness[0.007], Red}}]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell["And another:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(l = 2\ x\  + \ 3  y - 4  z; \nf\  = \ 2  x\^2 + 3  y\^2 - 6  z\^2; \n
    g = \ \(TL[Refl[l]]\)[f]; \n\n
    ImplicitPlot[{l == 0, f == 0, g == 0} /. z \[Rule] 1, \n
      \t{x, \(-3\), 3}, {y, \(-3\), 4}, \n\tPlotPoints\  -> \ 100, 
      PlotStyle 
        \[Rule] {{Thickness[0.007], Black}, \n\t\t\t{Thickness[0.007], Red}, 
          \n\t\ \ \ \t{Thickness[0.007], Red}}]; \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Seems fine.  (But it may not work for ",
  StyleBox["symbolic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " input!)  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.F.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Question.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Can you draw perpendiculars using a ",
  StyleBox["circle",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  "
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Answer:",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "The ",
  StyleBox["center",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of a circle is the ",
  StyleBox["pole",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the line at infinity.  Here is a general computation:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(ClearAll[f, g, a, b, c, Subscript]; 
    f = \((x - a\_1\ z)\)\^2 + \((y - b\_1\ z)\)\^2 - c\_1\%2\ z\^2; \n
    g = Envel[f]\), 
    \(Tidy[PD[z, g\/2]]\), 
    \(PD[f, a\_1\ u + b\_1\ v + w]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "So, according to the pole-polar definition, the point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(u\ a\_1 + v\ b\_1 + w\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is indeed the ",
  StyleBox["center",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " (as can be seen from the way the polynomial for ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is written).  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now, a well known property of a circle is that the ",
  StyleBox["tangents",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " at the ends of a diameter are ",
  StyleBox["perpendicular",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to the diameter.  Since we can find a diameter ",
  StyleBox["parallel",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to any given line, we can then find the ",
  StyleBox["pole",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of a diameter at infinity in order to pin down the perpendicular \
direction.  Keeping the above ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", its center ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\_1\ u + b\_1\ v + w\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and its envelope ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["g",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " fixed, we can make the following construction and test it:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(pc[p_, l_] := PE[p, PD[PE[a\_1\ u + b\_1\ v + w, PE[l, z]], g]]; \n\n
    Tidy/@Factor[pc[r\ u + s\ v + w, a\ x + b\ y + c\ z]]\n\), 
    \(Perp[r\ u + s\ v + w, a\ x + b\ y + c\ z]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The lines are the same \[LongDash] except for that factor of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(\(-2\) c\_1\%2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  But, if ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(c\_1 == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", the circle ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is ",
  StyleBox["degenerate",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " (that is, of radius ",
  StyleBox["zero",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ").  The construction of perpendiculars of course cannot work in that case. \
 Either way, we can use the idea of a circle to determine perpendicular \
lines.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.F.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Question.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "What about defining the notion of ",
  StyleBox["congruence",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " for ",
  StyleBox["segments",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "?"
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Answer.",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  Suppose we have two pairs ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{"{", 
          RowBox[{
            SubscriptBox[
              StyleBox["p",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], "1"], ",", \(q\_1\)}], "}"}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{"{", 
          RowBox[{
            SubscriptBox[
              StyleBox["p",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], "2"], ",", \(q\_2\)}], "}"}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " of ",
  StyleBox["distinct",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " points not at infinity.  (Equal points make segments of length ",
  StyleBox["zero",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  And those are only zero segments.  We skip this case.)  We then make a \
",
  StyleBox["parallelogram",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " using ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{"{", 
          RowBox[{\(p\_1\), ",", \(q\_1\), ",", 
            SubscriptBox[
              StyleBox["p",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], "2"]}], "}"}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", completing it to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{"{", 
          RowBox[{
            StyleBox[\(p\_1\),
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            StyleBox[",",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            StyleBox[\(q\_1\),
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            StyleBox[",",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            StyleBox[
              SubscriptBox[
                StyleBox["p",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], "2"],
              FontWeight->"Bold"], ",", \(q\_3\)}], "}"}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  In any parallelogram, the opposite sides are equal segments, so ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{"{", 
          RowBox[{
            SubscriptBox[
              StyleBox["p",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], "1"], ",", \(q\_1\)}], "}"}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is equal (as a segment) to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{"{", 
          RowBox[{
            SubscriptBox[
              StyleBox["p",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], "2"], ",", \(q\_3\)}], "}"}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Unfortunately, we do not have ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{\(q\_2\), "==", 
          SubscriptBox[
            StyleBox["q",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], "3"]}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", so we draw the ",
  StyleBox["circle",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " at center ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " passing through ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(q\_3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ". If the given point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(q\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " lies on this circle, we can say ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{"{", 
          RowBox[{
            SubscriptBox[
              StyleBox["p",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], "1"], ",", \(q\_1\)}], "}"}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is equal (as a segment) to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{"{", 
          RowBox[{
            SubscriptBox[
              StyleBox["p",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], "2"], ",", \(q\_2\)}], "}"}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Let's try to program this construction and draw a picture.  The main \
problem is ",
  StyleBox["how to determine the circle",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "?  Here follows one construction.  (The command itself in defined in ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(Implementations . nb\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".)"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        \(Circ[p_, q_] := 
          Module[{l, m, n, qq, rr}, \n\t\tl = PE[p, q]; \n\t\t
            qq = \ XP[p, PE[l, z], q, \(-1\)]; \n\t\tm = Perp[qq, l]; \n\t\t
            n = l + m; \n\t\trr = PE[n, Perp[q, n]]; \n\t\t
            P2P[u + I\ v, u - I\ v, \ q, qq, rr]]; \),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]],
    Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]]
}], "Text"],

Cell["Let's test it.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Circ[w, 5\ u + w] // Factor\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Clearly, unwanted factors may have to be eliminated.  Now, we can program \
a ",
  StyleBox["test",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " for congruence.  (The command itself in defined in ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(Implementations . nb\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".)"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        \(Cong[p_, q_, pp_, qq_] := 
          Module[{r, s, qqq, f}, \n\t\tr = PE[PE[p, q], z]; \n\t\t
            s = PE[PE[p, pp], z]; \n\t\tqqq = PE[PE[q, s], PE[pp, r]]; \n\t\t
            f = Circ[pp, qqq]; \n\t\tExpandAll[PD[f, qq\^2] == 0]]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]],
    Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]]
}], "Text"],

Cell["Some tests:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Cong[w, 3  u + w, \ 4  v + w, 7  v + w]\), 
    \(Cong[w, 3  u + w, \ 4  v + w, \(-7\) v + w]\), 
    \(Cong[w, 3  u + w, \ \(-4\) v + w, \(-7\) v + w]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "It seems to work!  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.F.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Euclidean properties of circles and parabolas", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Definition.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "A ",
  StyleBox["circle",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " is a non-degererate conic passing through the two circular points at \
infinity, ",
  StyleBox[Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(u \[PlusMinus] I\ v\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]]],
  ".  The ",
  StyleBox["center",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the circle is the pole of the line at infinity.  A ",
  StyleBox["diameter",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of a circle is a line passing through the center (which is the same as a \
polar of a point at infinity)."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We note that the ",
  StyleBox["diameter",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " determined by a circular point at infinity is the same as the ",
  StyleBox["tangent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " at that point, because a line through a circular point is perpendicular \
to ",
  StyleBox["itself",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell["We proved the following theorem in effect before:", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  The tangents at the intersection points of a diameter with a circle are \
perpendicular to the diameter.  Otherwise stated, the perpendicular (or ",
  StyleBox["normal",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ") to a tangent at a point on a circle is a diameter."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell["Here is another important property of a circle:", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Every circle is carried onto itself by ",
  StyleBox["reflection about a diameter",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " not passing through a circular point."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " Let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["l",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be a diameter, and let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["m",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be the prependicular at the center.  It is also a diameter.  The \
intersection points of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["m",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " with the circle harmonically separate the center from the point on ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["m",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " at infinity, and so the two points are ",
  StyleBox["interchanged",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " under ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(Refl[l]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", reflection about ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["l",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  But the points of intersection of  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["l",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " with the circle are ",
  StyleBox["left fixed",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " by the transformation ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(Refl[l]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Since the circle passes through the two circular points, and since \
\[LongDash] by definition \[LongDash] ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(Refl[l]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " interchanges these points, we have ",
  StyleBox["six",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " points of the given circle \[LongDash] no three collinear \[LongDash] \
which are all again mapped to points on the same circle.  Since it only takes \
",
  StyleBox["five",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " points to uniquely determine a conic, it follows that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(Refl[l]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " mapps the circle onto itself.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Recalling that the ",
  StyleBox["midpoint",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of a segment is the 4th harmonic with respect to the intersection of the \
line determined by the segment witht the line at infinity, the foregoing \
theorem justifies this conclusion: "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Corollary.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Any chord of a circle is ",
  StyleBox["bisected",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " by a perpendicular diameter."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  Reflection about the diameter has to interchange the endpoints of the \
chord.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
Let us turn some other kinds of conics.  Having fixed the line at \
infinity and the two circular points, the definition of a parabola and \
significant points and lines associated with it can be given in projective \
terms.  \
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Definition.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "A ",
  StyleBox["parabola",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " is a non-degererate conic ",
  StyleBox["tangent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to the line at infinity at a ",
  StyleBox["non-circular",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " point.  The ",
  StyleBox["focus",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the parabola is the intersection of the pair of other tangents from \
the circular points to the curve.  The ",
  StyleBox["directrix ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "of the parabola is the polar of the focus.  The ",
  StyleBox["axis",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the parabola is the join of the focus to the point of tangency at \
infinity.  The ",
  StyleBox["vertex",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the parabola is the other intersection of the axis with the curve."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "It is clear from the form of this definition that Euclidean \
transformations (whether direct or opposite) map parabolas to parabolas, \
carrying along their focii, axes, ",
  StyleBox["etc",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  We can also easily prove by projective transformations that a parabola \
has a ",
  StyleBox["symmetry property",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ":"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["  ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "Every parabola is carried onto itself by ",
  StyleBox["reflection about its axis",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  The two circular points and the focus form a ",
  StyleBox["circumscribed triangle",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " around the parabola.  The directrix, being the polar of the focus is also \
the join of the two points of tangency of the tangents from the circular \
points passing through the focus.  The point at infinity on the directrix has \
its polar passing through the focus.  However, that polar must pass through \
the point at infinity on the parabola.  (Why?)  So the polar ",
  StyleBox["is",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the axis.  Also the tangent at the vertex must be ",
  StyleBox["parallel",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to the directrix. (Why?)"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now, consider that the parabola is ",
  StyleBox["uniquely determined",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " by the two tangents (and points of tangency) joining the focus with the \
circular points together with the point at infinity where the parabola is \
tangent to the line at infinity.   So, if we take the projective \
transformation that interchanges the two tangents and points of tangency and \
interchanges the circular points, we will have a opposite ",
  StyleBox["Euclidean",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " transformation leaving the focus fixed.  It must map the parabola onto \
itself and map the directrix onto itself.  (Why?)  Hence, the vertex is left \
fixed and the point at infinity on the parabola is also fixed.  Thus, the \
axis is left ",
  StyleBox["pointwise fixed",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  But the point at infinity on the directrix and the point at infinity on \
the parabola must separate the circular points ",
  StyleBox["harmonically",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "; therefore, the diretrix is ",
  StyleBox["perpendicular",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to the axis.  (Why?) Since the directrix must be mapped onto itself by \
the projective transformation (Why?), this is enough to determine that the \
transformation ",
  StyleBox["is",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the reflection around the axis.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[
"And, note, in this proof we learned more about the vertex and directrix.  In \
addition, we can conclude \[LongDash] because the directrix is the polar of \
the focus \[LongDash] that the vertex is the midpoint of the segment on the \
axis between the focus and the intersection of the axis and the directrix."], 
  "Text"],

Cell["\<\
The other interesting properties of the parabola can be proved very \
geometrically from the definition above together with properties of general \
conics we have already established, but it is more expedient to use an \
equation in normal form.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Every parabola is equivalent by means of ",
  StyleBox["Euclidean transformations",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to one defined by an equation of the form:   ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox[\(y\ z\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["==",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[\(a\ x\^2\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " First, we take the general conic:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ClearAll[f, a, b, c, Subscript]; \n
    f = Table[a\_i, {i, 6}] . Bxyz[2]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "To be a parabola it is supposed to be tangent to the line at infinity.  \
Now, by a ",
  StyleBox["rotation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " we can move the point of tangency to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["v",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  That would mean that the following quantity must be ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["0",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ":"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PE[PD[f, v], z] // Expand\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "That forces two coefficients to be ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["0",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Next, by a ",
  StyleBox["translation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ",  we can move one point ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " at infinity to any other, and leave the points at infinity fixed:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(T = {{1, 0, 0}, {0, 1, 0}, {c, d, 1}}; \n\(TP[T]\)[u]\), 
    \(\(TP[T]\)[v]\), 
    \(\(TP[T]\)[w]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Therefore, we can make sure the parabola ",
  StyleBox["passes through",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["w",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "; and so:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[f, w\^2]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "But, we do not just want ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["w",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to be any point: it should be the ",
  StyleBox["vertex",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[". ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " Since the tangent at the vertex has to perpendicular to the axis of the \
parabola, the tangent has to be the ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axis.  Therefore, the following quantity has to be zero:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PE[PD[f, w], y] // Tidy\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "So, four coefficients are ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["0",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and the conic has this form (after dividing out a coefficient that \
cannot be ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["0",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and renaming the other one):"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f = 
      f /. {a\_1 \[Rule] \(-a\), a\_2 \[Rule] 0, a\_3 \[Rule] 0, 
          a\_4 \[Rule] 0, a\_5 \[Rule] 1, a\_6 \[Rule] 0}\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Note that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["a",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is not ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["0",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Now a ",
  StyleBox["similitude",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " could multiply both ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["x",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["y",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " by the same factor, but then ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(x\^2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is multiplied by the ",
  StyleBox["square",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the factor.  After dividing out the factor it will only be left in the \
 ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(x\^2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " term.  The factor ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(1\/a\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " could be used for example.  So after the similitude, we could get to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(\(-x\^2\) + y\ z\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", which is the ",
  StyleBox["standard parabola",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  But we will leave the quantity ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["a",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " in the equation, since the rotation and the translation did not change \
the scale.  This gives us the normal form we sought.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Another way of phrasing the last theorem is to say that ",
  StyleBox["all parabolas are similar under Euclidean transformations.",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Question.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Where is the focus of the parabola ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox[\(y\ z\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[" ",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["==",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[" ",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[
          RowBox[{"a", " ", 
            SuperscriptBox[
              StyleBox["x",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], "2"]}],
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]],
  "?"
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Answer:",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  First we need to find where the tangents from the circular points meet \
the parabola."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(ClearAll[f, a, b, c, d, e, Subscript]; \nf = \(-a\)\ x\^2 + y\ z; \n
    Solve[{f == 0, PD[f, u + I\ v] == 0, z == 1}, {x, y, z}]\), 
    \(Solve[{f == 0, PD[f, u - I\ v] == 0, z == 1}, {x, y, z}]\)}], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Next, we have to claculate the intersection of the two \
tangents:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PE[PE[u + I\ v, I\/\(2\ a\)\ u - 1\/\(4\ a\)\ v + w], 
          PE[u - I\ v, \(-\(I\/\(2\ a\)\)\)\ u - 1\/\(4\ a\)\ v + w]]/
        \((\(-2\) I)\) // Tidy\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "And we found the focus to be the point ",
  StyleBox[Cell[BoxData[
      \(v\/\(4  a\) + w\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"]],
  StyleBox["."],
  "   ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.F.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
The next theorem explains why the focus of the parabola is so \
called.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " Lines parallel to the axis of the parabola are ",
  StyleBox["reflected",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " by the tangent at the point of intersection to lines passing through the \
focus."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "First, we need to parameterize the points of the parabola."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ClearAll[f, s, t, par, \ refl]; \nf = \(-a\)\ x\^2 + y\ z; \n
    par[s_, t_] := t\ s\ u + a\ t\^2\ v + s\^2\ w; \nPD[f, par[s, t]\^2]\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Next, we calculate the ",
  StyleBox["tangent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", the ",
  StyleBox["normal",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " (= perpendicular to the tangent), and the ",
  StyleBox["reflection",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the line parallel to the axis:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(tan[s_, t_] := PD[f, par[s, t]]; \n\n
    norm[s_, t_] := Tidy[Last[Factor[Perp[par[s, t], tan[s, t]]]]]; \n\n
    refl[s_, t_] := 
      Tidy[Last[
          Factor[XL[tan[s, t], norm[s, t], PE[v, par[s, t]], \(-1\)]]]]; \ 
    \)\)], "Input"],

Cell["Here are the formulae for the three lines just defined:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(tan[s, t]\), 
    \(norm[s, t]\), 
    \(refl[s, t]\)}], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Now we test whether the reflected line passes through the \
focus:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[refl[s, t], v\/\(4\ a\) + w]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "It works.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
Here is a picture showing several rays and their reflections.\
\>", 
  "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(t\_1 = Table[PE[v, par[s, t/2]] == 0, {t, 1, 5}]; 
    t\_2 = Table[refl[s, t/2] == 0, {t, 1, 5}]; \n
    ImplicitPlot[
      Join[{f == 0}, t\_1, t\_2] /. {s \[Rule] 1, a \[Rule] 1/2, 
          z \[Rule] 1}, {x, \(-4\), 4}, {y, \(-2\), 4}]; \)], "Input"],

Cell["Next, we establish another important property.", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  The for a parabola, a ",
  StyleBox["point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " on the curve, the ",
  StyleBox["focus",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", the ",
  StyleBox["foot",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the perpendicular to the directrix, and the ",
  StyleBox["intersection",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the tangent with the axis form a ",
  StyleBox["rhombus",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Therefore, the parabola is the locus of points ",
  StyleBox["equidistant",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " from the focus and the directrix."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  A rhombus is a parallelogram with ",
  StyleBox["four equal sides",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  (The opposite sides of any parallelogram are always equal.)  A rhombus \
is also a parallelogram with ",
  StyleBox["perpendicular diagonals",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[". ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox["(T",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "he diagonals of any parallelogram always bisect each other.)  So, we have \
to prove two special lines are perpendicular.  Here is the tangent at a \
point:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(tan[s, t]\)], "Input"],

Cell["Here is the directrix:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[f, \ v\/\(4\ a\) + w]\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Here is the line joining the focus to the intersection of the line \
parallel to the axis (= the perpendicular to the directrix through the \
point):\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PE[v\/\(4\ a\) + w, PE[PE[v, par[s, t]], y + z\/\(4\ a\)]] // Factor
      \)], "Input"],

Cell["And here is the perpendicular to the tangent from the focus:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Perp[v\/\(4\ a\) + w, tan[s, t]] // Factor\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "It is the same line!  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell["We can draw a picture to illustrate the theorem.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[{f == 0, y + z\/\(4\ a\) == 0, \n\t\ttan[s, t] == 0, \n
          \t\tPE[v\/\(4\ a\) + w, par[s, t]] == 0, \n\t\t
          PE[v, par[s, t]] == 0, \n\t\t
          PE[PE[x, tan[s, t]], PE[y + z\/\(4\ a\), PE[v, par[s, t]]]] == 0, \n
          \tPE[v\/\(4\ a\) + w, PE[y + z\/\(4\ a\), PE[v, par[s, t]]]] == 
            0} /. {s \[Rule] 1, t \[Rule] 2.4, a \[Rule] 1\/3, z \[Rule] 1}, 
      \n\t{x, \(-4\), 5}, {y, \(-3\), 5}]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
It is obvious that the two perpendicular lines intersect on the \
tangent at the vertex.  (Why?)  There is another interesting property \
involving tangents and the directrix.  This time we draw a picture \
first:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[{f == 0, y + z\/\(4\ a\) == 0, \n\t\ttan[s, t] == 0, \n\t
          Perp[\tPE[y + z\/\(4\ a\), tan[s, t]], tan[s, t]] == 0} /. {
          s \[Rule] 1, t \[Rule] 2.4, a \[Rule] 1\/3, z \[Rule] 1}, \n
      \t{x, \(-4\), 5}, {y, \(-3\), 5}]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell["Ah, ha!  We should try to prove:", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  The ",
  StyleBox["directrix",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of a parabola is the locus of the intersections of pairs of ",
  StyleBox["perpendicular tangents",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to the curve."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  First, we compute the envelope:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(g = Envel[f]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[
"Then \[LongDash] as in the diagram \[LongDash] we check that the \
perpendicular line is indeed a tangent."], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[Perp[\tPE[y + z\/\(4\ a\), tan[s, t]], tan[s, t]]\^2, g]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "It is!  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Question.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  What if we reflected rays ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " parallel to the axis off the parabola?"
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Answer:",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " It seems unlikely that we can expect the reflected rays all to pass \
through a point.  Perhaps, however, they are all tangent to a nice curve.  To \
check this out, we have to find a formula for the rays.  Let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(b\ u + c\ v\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be the ponit at infinity through which they all pass.  The ray coming \
from there to the point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(par[s, t]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is therefore ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PE[b\ u + c\ v, par[s, t]]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  The reflected ray is then computed as follows:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(refl2[s_, t_] := 
      Map[Factor, 
        Tidy[Last[
            Factor[XL[tan[s, t], norm[s, t], PE[b\ u + c\ v, par[s, t]], 
                \(-1\)]]]], {2}]; \nrefl2[s, t]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "A little complicated, being a ",
  StyleBox["4",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "th-degree parameterization.  We can figure out what the algebraic \
realtionship between the coordinates of such a family of rays is by using the \
",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["Eliminate",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " command."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(h = 
      Tidy[{1, \(-1\)} . 
          List@@Eliminate[{
                s\^2\ \((c\ s\^2 - 4\ a\ b\ s\ t - 4\ a\^2\ c\ t\^2)\) == u, 
                s\^2\ \((b\ s\^2 + 4\ a\ c\ s\ t - 4\ a\^2\ b\ t\^2)\) == v, 
                \(-t\)\ \((c\ s\^3 - 3\ a\ b\ s\^2\ t - 4\ a\^3\ b\ t\^3)
                      \) == w}, {s, t}]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "It could be worse!  Unfortunately, ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " could not compute the dual ",
  StyleBox["xyz",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-curve with these symbolic coefficients.  So, we have to be content with a \
special case:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(k = 
      Dual[Envel[Dual[h /. {a \[Rule] 1\/3, b \[Rule] 1, c \[Rule] \(-1\)}]]]
        \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "It is only a cubic!  Since the envelope ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["h",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " was found by a parameterization, it is a good guess that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["k",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is ",
  StyleBox["singular",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  We will know in a moment when we draw a picture."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(rays\_1 = Table[PE[b\ u + c\ v, par[s, t\/2]] == 0, {t, 1, 8}]; 
    rays\_2 = Table[refl2[s, t\/2] == 0, {t, 1, 8}]; 
    ImplicitPlot[
      Join[{f == 0, k == 0}, rays\_1, rays\_2] /. {a \[Rule] 1\/3, 
          b \[Rule] 1, c \[Rule] \(-1\), s \[Rule] 1, z \[Rule] 1}, \n
      \t{x, \(-12\), 6}, {y, \(-2\), 10}, \n\tPlotPoints \[Rule] 100, \n\t
      PlotStyle \[Rule] 
        Join[{{Thickness[0.006], Blue}, {Thickness[0.006], Red}}, 
          Table[Black, {16}]]]; \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "It looks as if the (singular) cubic is ",
  StyleBox["tangent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to the parabola.  Perhaps we can check this by finding the points of \
intersection of the two curves."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(sol = 
      Solve[{f == 0, k == 0, z == 1} /. {a \[Rule] 1\/3, b \[Rule] 1, 
            c \[Rule] \(-1\), s \[Rule] 1}, {x, y, z}]\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Part of that looks OK.  We can get a better idea if we check the \
numerical values.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(N[sol] // MatrixForm\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Good!  ",
  StyleBox["Six",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " intersections.  The double one ought to be the the point of tangency (and \
we will check that), there are two complex intersections, and there are two \
other real ones \[LongDash] one of which we have already seen in the above \
picture.  Here is a snapshot of the two curves to show that other real \
intersection:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[{f == 0, k == 0} /. {a \[Rule] 1\/3, b \[Rule] 1, 
          c \[Rule] \(-1\), z \[Rule] 1}, \n
      \t{x, \(-20\), 20}, {y, \(-10\), 100}, \n\tPlotPoints \[Rule] 200, \n\t
      PlotStyle \[Rule] {{Thickness[0.006], Blue}, {Thickness[0.006], Red}}]; 
    \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[
"Finally, we check the point of tangency \[LongDash] after setting the \
parameters in the two curves."], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f = f /. {a \[Rule] 1\/3}; \n
    k = \(-\(k\/7776\)\) /. {a \[Rule] 1\/3, b \[Rule] 1, c \[Rule] \(-1\)}; 
    \np = \(-\(3\/2\)\) u + \(3\/4\) v + w; \)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(PD[f, p] // Tidy\), 
    \(PD[f, p\^2] // Tidy\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(PD[k, p\^2] // Tidy\), 
    \(PD[k, p\^3] // Tidy\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Very good!  The two curves have common tangents at the point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " of intersection; hence, they are tangent.  That is probably enough about \
parabolas!  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.F.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Question.",
  StyleBox[
  " What happends if we reflect a bundle of parallel lines off a circular \
mirror?",
    FontWeight->"Plain"]
}], "Text",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Answer:",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " We work as before, using now a parameterization of the unit circle this \
time.  We will take rays passing through the point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(u + v\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " at infinity."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(circ[s_, t_] := \ 
      2\ s\ t\ u + \((s\^2 - t\^2)\)\ v + \((s\^2 + t\^2)\)\ w; \n
    tan[s_, t_] := PD[x\^2 + y\^2 - z\^2, circ[s, t]]; \n
    norm[s_, t_] := Tidy[Last[Factor[Perp[circ[s, t], tan[s, t]]]]]; \n
    refl[s_, t_] := 
      Tidy[Last[
          Factor[XL[tan[s, t], norm[s, t], PE[u + v, circ[s, t]], \(-1\)]]]]; 
    \)], "Input"],

Cell["We note that all the normals pass through the origin:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(norm[s, t]\)], "Input"],

Cell["The reflected rays are a bit more complex, however:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(refl[s, t]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Map[Factor, %, {2}]\)], "Input"],

Cell["We then find by elimination the envelope they touch:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(caus = 
      Tidy[{1, \(-1\)} . 
          List@@Eliminate[{
                \((s\^4 + 4\ s\^3\ t - 6\ s\^2\ t\^2 - 4\ s\ t\^3 + t\^4)
                    \) == u, 
                \((s\^4 - 4\ s\^3\ t - 6\ s\^2\ t\^2 + 4\ s\ t\^3 + t\^4)
                    \) == v, 
                \((s\^2 - 2\ s\ t - t\^2)\)\ \((s\^2 + t\^2)\) == w\ }, {s, 
                t}]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "That is of the ",
  StyleBox["4",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "th degree, but what of the dual curve?"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(dual = Dual[Envel[Dual[caus]]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "That is a ",
  StyleBox["6",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "th-degree curve, and here is its position realtive to the given circle:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[{x\^2 + y\^2 - z\^2 == 0, dual == 0} /. {z \[Rule] 1}, {
        x, \(-2\), 2}, {y, \(-2\), 2}, PlotPoints \[Rule] 200, \n\t
      PlotStyle \[Rule] {{Thickness[0.006], Blue}, {Thickness[0.006], Red}}]; 
    \)\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
And we can show a selection of the rays and their reflections:\
\>",
   "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(rays\_1 = Table[PE[u + v, circ[1, t/2]] == 0, {t, \(-12\), \(-1\)}]; 
    rays\_2 = Table[refl[1, t/2] == 0, {t, \(-12\), \(-1\)}]; \n\n
    ImplicitPlot[
      Join[{x\^2 + y\^2 - z\^2 == 0, dual == 0}, rays\_1, rays\_2] /. {
          z \[Rule] 1}, \n\t{x, \(-1.2\), 1.2}, {y, \(-1.2\), 1.2}, \n\t
      PlotPoints \[Rule] 100, 
      PlotStyle \[Rule] 
        Join[{{Thickness[0.006], Blue}, {Thickness[0.006], Red}}, 
          Table[Black, {24}]]]; \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Such curves are called ",
  StyleBox["caustics",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", and in particular cases for the circle and the parbola we have been able \
to find their equations.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.F.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]]
},
FrontEndVersion->"X 3.0",
ScreenRectangle->{{0, 1280}, {0, 1024}},
